<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Sixtus IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1414–1484</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Francesco della Rovere</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Sixtus IV reigned from 1471 to 1484. He built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and refounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>, but was notorious for advancing his nephews, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a>, for whom he acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a> and Forlì. His family's hostility to the Medici lay behind the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Sixtus IV" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Berruguete · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_Berruguete_-_Portrait_of_Pope_Sixtus_IV_della_Rovere_-_1916.815_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_ArtFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
